{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "Amtrak's Abraham Lincoln and Ann Rutledge, the two Chicago-St. Louis names Amtrak inherited from the Gulf, Mobile and Ohio on May 1 1971. The Alton Railroad had launched the streamlined Abraham Lincoln in 1935 and added the Ann Rutledge as its companion in 1937, named for the woman from New Salem who may have been Lincoln's first love; for their pre-Amtrak history see alton_gmo_abraham_lincoln.geojson. Amtrak dropped the Abraham Lincoln name in 1978. The Ann Rutledge lasted far longer: through Chicago-Kansas City by way of St. Louis, cut back to a St. Louis-Kansas City train in 2006, and finally folded into the Missouri River Runner in January 2009 after 72 years. Chicago-Kansas City through running returned in May 2022 under the combined name Lincoln Service Missouri River Runner.", "sources": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abraham_Lincoln_(train) (operators listed as B&O/Alton 1935-1947, GM&O 1947-1971, Amtrak 1971-1977; the article's category gives the service as discontinued in 1978)", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ann_Rutledge_(train) (the Alton inaugurated the Ann Rutledge in 1937 as a companion to the Abraham Lincoln over the St. Louis-Chicago route; Amtrak ran it St. Louis-Kansas City as part of the Missouri Services brand; in 2009 Amtrak consolidated the Ann Rutledge, Kansas City Mule and St. Louis Mule under the name Missouri River Runner; in May 2022 Amtrak returned the Chicago-Kansas City via St. Louis route as the Lincoln Service Missouri River Runner)", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Missouri_River_Runner and https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mules_(train) (in July 1971 the St. Louis-Kansas City route became the western leg of the National Limited; when that was discontinued in 1979 the Ann Rutledge became the only Chicago-Kansas City train via St. Louis; its eastern terminus was cut back to St. Louis in 2006; the Kansas City Mule and St. Louis Mule began Oct 26 1980; consolidation into the Missouri River Runner came in January 2009)"], "not_yet_sourced": "Headways are placeholders: no Amtrak timetable has been consulted for either train, and the daily round-trip counts are not established. Sources conflict on when Amtrak dropped the Abraham Lincoln name -- 1977 per the article infobox, 1978 per its category -- and 1978 is used. The exact 2006 date of the Ann Rutledge's cutback to St. Louis and the exact January 2009 consolidation date are not established beyond the year and month. Geometry is the GTFS route shape rather than a traced alignment; see geometry_quality."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Amtrak", "line": "Amtrak: Abraham Lincoln", "id": "amtrak-abraham-lincoln", "color": "#1c4e80", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 85, "speed_source": "estimated: Amtrak corridor service over the 284-mile Chicago-St. Louis route; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1971, "end": 1978, "headway_min": 720, "note": "Amtrak took the Abraham Lincoln over from the Gulf, Mobile and Ohio on May 1 1971 and kept the name on the Chicago-St. Louis run. Sources disagree on the end: the Wikipedia article's infobox gives Amtrak operation as 1971-1977, while its category says the service was discontinued in 1978. 1978 is used here and the conflict is recorded. The corridor continues as Amtrak's Lincoln Service - see amtrak.geojson.", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abraham_Lincoln_(train)"]}], "geometry_source": "Geometry is the modern Amtrak GTFS-derived alignment for this corridor, taken from the 'Lincoln Service Missouri River Runner' feature in amtrak.geojson, which covers Chicago\u2013St. Louis\u2013Kansas City \u2014 the same physical route these trains ran. Sliced at the vertex nearest St. Louis Gateway station for the St. Louis\u2013Kansas City extent.", "geometry_quality": "NOT A TRACED ALIGNMENT: this is 